#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:3
#: 9e7d0e58a1984a2a97d8ecbcd1a26c1b
msgid "ARCHIVED: See :ref:`common-geofencing-landing-page` for info for firmware versions 4.1 and later"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:7
#: 600e61b850354f74b35eed1c85cae5eb
msgid "Geo-Fencing in Plane"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:9
#: 4303014645f349a49ceb384f861ecc3f
msgid "The Geo-Fencing support in Plane firmware versions prior to 4.1, allows you to set a virtual 'fence' around the area you want to fly in, specified as an enclosed polygon of GPS positions plus a minimum and maximum altitude. For firmware versions 4.1 and later, Plane uses the either a home centered Cylindrical Fence, see :ref:`common-ac2_simple_geofence` and/or Inclusion/Exclusion Fences, see :ref:`common-polygon_fence`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:14
#: 8af3792248924553a6b6ae2c005c0a8d
msgid "In Plane you MUST have the fence polygon drawn for the geofence parameters to work.  In Copter you can specify a maximum fence altitude without the fence polygon and Copter will adhere to it but Plane requires you to draw the polygon for any of the fence parameters including the max/min ALT to work."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:20
#: 60910496802d4667be4eaa3d1330d72b
msgid "When fencing is enabled, if your plane goes outside the fenced area then it will switch to GUIDED mode, and will fly back to a pre-defined return point, and loiter there ready for you to take over again. You then use a switch on your transmitter or use commands in your Ground Control Station (GCS) to take back control."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:30
#: e534ac6499524c1dafe0281d0f513ff9
msgid "Use for R/C training"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:32
#: f4d2f24efcf2407c83eee814e016f5da
msgid "One of the main uses of geo-fencing is to teach yourself (or someone else) to fly radio controlled planes. When you have a properly configured geo-fence it is very hard to crash, and you can try manoeuvres that would normally be too likely to end in a crash, trusting the APM to 'bounce' the plane off the geo-fence before the flight ends in disaster."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:39
#: 0236dd9feaac483f83c504ae02a96a5c
msgid "Geo-fencing can be combined with any flight mode. So for a raw beginner, you would combine it with one of the stabilised flight modes (such as STABILIZE or FBWA). Once the pilot has gained some confidence you could combine it with MANUAL mode, which gives direct control of the plane and allows for the most interesting aerobatic manoeuvres. When used in this way the autopilot stays out of your way completely, just passing the controls to the servos directly, and only takes control if you go outside the fenced area or outside the defined altitude range."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:49
#: d46a80ca05954fbd9880b3630eb210f7
msgid "Use for containment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:51
#: 9d5a89147755469d8da41e30611fffb8
msgid "During fully autonomous operation the fence can be used as a failsafe measure to ensure the aircraft stays within the intended flight area. During fully autonomous operation use the :ref:`FENCE_AUTOENABLE<FENCE_AUTOENABLE>` parameter and the plane will automatically engage the fence after takeoff is complete and automatically disable the fence when it arrives at a landing waypoint.  Set the :ref:`FENCE_AUTOENABLE<FENCE_AUTOENABLE>` parameter to 1 to use this feature.  For details on setting up landing and takeoff waypoints see the :ref:`Planning a Mission with Waypoints and Events <common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events>` page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:61
#: 3bf1633fd7c444bfac0009c50547d4a0
msgid "Of course, the fence can be used for containment in semi-autonomous missions as well (missions where, e.g., takeoff and/or landing are manual) -- the fence would still be enabled/disabled via the R/C transmitter or the GCS in that case -- :ref:`FENCE_AUTOENABLE<FENCE_AUTOENABLE>` is optional."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:67
#: 9e1c323a064648c6a35ae240381f71fa
msgid "Setting up geo-fencing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:69
#: e58e9bb41af84ab2a48a16777ee4d9cf
msgid "To setup geo-fencing in Plane you need to configure several things:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:71
#: c5f7722b32184fbabeb0d72384e331f5
msgid "the boundary of the fence, as a set of GPS points"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:72
#: def33393678f49649f3d993816cea475
msgid "the action to take on fence breach"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:73
#: 68eefcd912c847679dbb0cd7f6cf4c30
msgid "the location of the return point; note that optionally you may use the :ref:`FENCE_RET_RALLY<FENCE_RET_RALLY>` parameter to have the plane return to the closest :ref:`Rally Point <common-rally-points>` instead of the fence return point."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:77
#: 6e4b870e2fc244a58463f299ae0badc6
msgid "the minimum and maximum altitude of the fenced area"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:78
#: f49652f355e7493ca4fd41ee1ce78322
msgid "what RC channel on your transmitter you will use to enable geo-fencing (if any)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:80
#: 181563de95c74a77acadbd2d5538af74
msgid "an optional setting (:ref:`FENCE_AUTOENABLE<FENCE_AUTOENABLE>`) when you want to configure the fence to automatically enable after an autonomous takeoff and automatically disable after an autonomous landing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:83
#: 2bb4320221ec47d8bc7164861f2ac14a
msgid "how you want to take back control after a fence breach"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:85
#: d9ec534ba0fd497e9f35ac4af2bf5ab8
msgid "These can all be setup using Mission Planner."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:87
#: 892c583c417945888e3cc771523f6e85
msgid "There are a few rules that you must follow when setting up your fence boundary:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:90
#: fbe8e360ed8d421bbac8141ee348a32b
msgid "the return point must be inside the fence boundary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:91
#: f0adff9674534f7992186e9b11d905cd
msgid "the fence boundary must be fully enclosed. This means it must have at least 4 points, and the last point must be the same as the first point"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:94
#: 9863ceb7729a4f0590ee71e28d2aaa54
msgid "the boundary can have at most 18 points"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:96
#: 842b9dc284ec4fdb8b896d6bc8faa579
msgid "If you setup your fence with the APM planner it should ensure you follow these rules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:99
#: 7885cd2c75614054bdcd6e1ca0d7915b
msgid "Please remember when making your fence boundary that your plane will have some momentum when it hits the fence, and will take time to turn back to the return point. For a plane like the SkyWalker we recommend an additional safety margin of around 30 meters inside the true boundary of where you want to fly. The same goes for the minimum altitude - you need to make it high enough that the APM has time to recover from a fast dive. How much margin you need depends on the flight characteristics of your plane."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:108
#: 76d13db2dd604934bbe3f187310ffac2
msgid "Apart from the fence boundary, the following MAVLink parameters control geo-fencing behaviour:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:111
#: 2ad8bbe8d1864c66baff11257ae5cae1
msgid ":ref:`FENCE_ACTION<FENCE_ACTION>` - the action to take on fence breach. This defaults to zero which disables geo-fencing. Set it to 1 to enable geo-fencing and fly to the return point on fence breach.  Set to 2 to report a breach to the GCS but take no other action.  Set to 3 to have the plane head to the return point on breach, but the pilot will maintain manual throttle control in this case."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:117
#: ad92a115168648be89d18b4f556462b6
msgid ":ref:`FENCE_ALT_MIN<FENCE_ALT_MIN>` - the minimum altitude in meters. If this is zero then you will not have a minimum altitude."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:119
#: 72cc1e8873674f0880cfeafe4daaa2ec
msgid ":ref:`FENCE_ALT_MAX<FENCE_ALT_MAX>` - the maximum altitude in meters. If this is zero then you will not have a maximum altitude."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:121
#: 896a254f888e40738cf89130773426ea
msgid "``FENCE_CHANNEL<FENCE_CHANNEL>`` (revs previous to 4.1), ``RCx_OPTION`` = 11 - the RC input channel to watch for enabling the geo-fence. This defaults to zero, which disables geo-fencing. You should set it to a spare RC input channel that is connected to a two position switch on your transmitter. Fencing will be enabled when this channel goes above a PWM value of 1750. If your transmitter supports it it is also a good idea to enable audible feedback when this channel is enabled (a beep every few seconds), so you can tell if the fencing is enabled without looking down."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:129
#: bb1d6211af254a91b1951ab021c83142
msgid ":ref:`FENCE_TOTAL<FENCE_TOTAL>` - the number of points in your fence (the return point plus the enclosed boundary). This should be set for you by the planner when you create the fence."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:132
#: ffbe2aaafab3491fb89bbd91ef46d16b
msgid "``FENCE_RETALT`` - the altitude the aircraft will fly at when flying to the return point and when loitering at the return point (in meters). Note that when :ref:`FENCE_RET_RALLY<FENCE_RET_RALLY>` is set to 1 this parameter is ignored and the loiter altitude of the closest :ref:`Rally Point <common-rally-points>` is used instead.  If this parameter is zero and :ref:`FENCE_RET_RALLY<FENCE_RET_RALLY>` is also zero, the midpoint of the :ref:`FENCE_ALT_MAX <FENCE_ALT_MAX>` and :ref:`FENCE_ALT_MIN <FENCE_ALT_MIN>` parameters is used as the return altitude."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:139
#: 57637317779e461b9a2750d3dbd432ff
msgid ":ref:`FENCE_AUTOENABLE<FENCE_AUTOENABLE>` - if set to 1, the aircraft will boot with the fence disabled.  After an autonomous takeoff completes the fences will automatically enable.  When the autonomous mission arrives at a landing waypoint the fence automatically disables."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:143
#: 3706514d29294d39b1ce392456182392
msgid ":ref:`FENCE_RET_RALLY<FENCE_RET_RALLY>` - if set to 1 the aircraft will head to the nearest :ref:`Rally Point <common-rally-points>` rather than the fence return point when the fence is breached. Note that the loiter altitude of the Rally Point is used as the return altitude."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:151
#: c648a4529042484790d6b3467ec68e5e
msgid "A Rally Point can be outside of the geofence but this is NOT recommended.  If you have a rally point outside the geofence you will need to disable the geofence using ``FENCE_CHANNEL`` before you can control the plane again otherwise the plane will stay in GUIDED mode FOREVER circling the rally point.  Once the geofence is disabled you should fly the plane back inside the geofence and then re-enable it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:159
#: 6a8bb68f492d4a98bea6a29a2f20dbf3
msgid "One additional parameter may be useful to get the most out of geo-fencing. When you breach the fence, the plane will switch to GUIDED mode and fly back to the return point (or the nearest Rally Point, if :ref:`FENCE_RET_RALLY<FENCE_RET_RALLY>` has been set to 1). Once you are back inside the fence boundary you are able to take control again, and you need to tell the APM that you want to take control. You can do that in one of 3 ways:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:166
#: e5a57cef8f9245f58b0dd91b850ee332
msgid "changing modes using the mode switch on your transmitter, or changing modes via the Mission Planner GCS (e.g., change from GUIDED mode to AUTO mode)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:169
#: 7fc3e6594dbc416c8f30cfcfe5b6f420
msgid "disabling and re-enabling geo-fencing using the rc channel setup for fence control above."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:171
#: c32b7541a7d6489eaabfe75e4fbe5e70
msgid "set the ``RST_SWITCH_CH`` MAVLink parameter to another two-position channel that is attached to a spring loaded switch. The ``RST_SWITCH`` parameter defaults to zero which disables it. If you set it to a channel then you can use this channel switch to take back control after a fence breach."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:177
#: cff890fbfd9f47bb8fea4587f38b3102
msgid "If not flying completely autonomously, I find that using ``RST_SWITCH_CH`` is the best option for geo-fencing as it means that the APM has fencing enabled throughout the flight, and you don't get any behaviour change by switching modes. It does take up another channel though, so some people may not have enough channels to use it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:184
#: 4911a6446d7c44eca1f1564b14e59f90
msgid "Setting up the fence boundary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:187
#: 50b266a820384c3a96bea55437679e60
msgid "APMPlanner"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:189
#: 68788ab123224dffb890b9f8cb99662a
msgid "To setup a fence boundary you should use the 'Flight Planner' screen in the APM Planner."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:192
#: 99fd0936dd4b48a48f1e8c0abb0fedd6
msgid "Start by right-clicking the location you want for the return point and choosing 'Set return location'. The return point should be somewhere in the middle of your flight area, and in easy visual range of where you will be standing when you fly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:197
#: 5604eeaa8d5743f8b9b82dce8ec55c40
msgid "After you've set the return point you should right click on the first point on the boundary of the fence you want. Choose 'Draw Polygon ->"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:200
#: 96362d16758247709dd57ca30e47d2f8
msgid "Add polygon point'. You are then in polygon mode, and you should left-click to add each point in the boundary of your fence. The planner will automatically complete the polygon by connecting the last point to the first one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:205
#: a529e4144d89473eba5e383828547b6e
msgid "You can then right-click and choose geo-fencing upload to send your fence boundary to the APM. The planner will ask you for the minimum and maximum altitude (in meters) of your fence before uploading. You can also save your fence to a file for later loading."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:211
#: 9b78bea303a44c4eb363f568ec1efd50
msgid "Mission Planner"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:213
#: c24714e7429d46a898f2e4bcd9745c5a
msgid "Mission Planner follows a very similar process.  Start by right-clicking where you want to begin the geo-fence boundary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:219
#: f5fc5967eec04773bb4d75b639dec3c8
msgid "Add Polygon Point"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:221
#: e89e752c784441d3a1eb5bc55e1d255f
msgid "Continue to click on the map where you want the geo-fence boundary and the polygon will appear.  You can drag any points you want to adjust. Then right click on the map where you want the plane to return to when a geo-fence breach occurs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:229
#: f6101d892bef4a09ac0ab6bdef58d298
msgid "Geo-Fence Set ReturnLocation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:231
#: 99351b92efe44f398a5aee401623c4b5
msgid "Finally upload the geo-fence."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:236
#: c22cc86c57c4482486f371ba03442b62
msgid "Geo-Fence Upload"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:239
#: 1bee299336f643bc9f059f7a32a6e27f
msgid "Altitude of the return point"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:241
#: 1aff6bd7b32847a5bab1d96a606f3d4a
msgid "If you set the :ref:`FENCE_RET_RALLY<FENCE_RET_RALLY>` parameter to 1, then the return altitude will be **the same as the loiter altitude of the nearest Rally Point**.  If the :ref:`FENCE_RET_RALLY<FENCE_RET_RALLY>` is set to 0, then you may set the return altitude in meters above the Home Point with the ``FENCE_RETALT`` parameter.  **Otherwise**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:247
#: 97d177ea3378456db5e2038067d1a045
msgid "If you set ``FENCE_MINALT`` and ``FENCE_MAXALT`` to other than zero (and have ``FENCE_MAXALT`` greater than FENCE_MINALT) then the return point altitude will be half way between ``FENCE_MINALT`` and ``FENCE_MAXALT``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:251
#: 1810292bed1041ddb56341c6a77f3918
msgid "If you don't setup ``FENCE_MINALT`` and ``FENCE_MAXALT`` (ie. leave them at zero) then the return point altitude will be given by the :ref:`RTL_ALTITUDE<RTL_ALTITUDE>` parameter, which is also used for RTL mode. Note that :ref:`RTL_ALTITUDE<RTL_ALTITUDE>` is in centimetres, whereas ``FENCE_MINALT`` and ``FENCE_MAXALT`` are in meters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:256
#: 605716ae7733493b9bae9e87ffa5dfe7
msgid "If your flying club and local flying rules don't set a maximum altitude then we recommend you use a maximum altitude of at most 122 meters (which is around 400 feet). Beyond that altitude it becomes quite difficult to keep good eye contact with your model."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:261
#: 041e67ca5aba4e669eaf07d576501fdd
msgid "With ``FENCE_MINALT`` set at 30 meters (to allow for some dive momentum) and ``FENCE_MAXALT`` set to 122 meters, the return point will be 76 meters, which is quite a good altitude to leave the plane loitering while you are getting ready to have another go."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:267
#: c467b2a5cbc0499db88bfc46c9ab2633
msgid "Stick-mixing on fence breach"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:269
#: 57be85e63df542be92f7b13a072f905b
msgid "ArduPilot enables 'stick mixing' by default when in auto modes. This means that you can change the path of a loiter, for example, by using your transmitter sticks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:273
#: 831f440ac30c4b55a859540065eeb9f4
msgid "When you are using geo-fencing, stick mixing will be disabled on fence breach until your plane is back inside the fenced region. This is to ensure that the bad control inputs that caused you to breach the fence don't prevent it from recovering to the return point."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:278
#: 6ddb6294a1a64b07b474df084ae76f5c
msgid "As soon as you are back inside the fence stick mixing will be re-enabled, allowing you to control the GUIDED mode that the plane will be in. If by using stick mixing you manage to take the plane outside the fence again then stick mixing will again be disabled until you are back inside the fence."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:285
#: a51255e27a42410f8efc7048a3c61d96
msgid "Tips for flying with geo-fencing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:287
#: 9e578a4800994a449df2ca3dac129011
msgid "You should have geo-fencing disabled when on the ground and for takeoff. Be careful not to enable it on the ground, as it may declare a fence breach and try to fly to the return point.  If flying fully autonomously you may use the :ref:`FENCE_AUTOENABLE<FENCE_AUTOENABLE>` parameter to assist with this complication."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:293
#: 9f0ede9e74464bb1af1fbba08f67b674
msgid "Also remember to disable it for landing, as the altitude breach when you are coming in will make it very hard to land!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:296
#: 47ac4bdaff4c433e817c164bf740cf92
msgid "If you are using an APM1 and want to combine geo-fencing with MANUAL mode, then remember that on the APM1 the APM software is bypassed when using channel 8 for mode switching and a switch PWM channel value above 1750 (this is called 'hardware manual' on the APM1). So you either need to set a different switch position as MANUAL, or use a different mode switch control channel (and set :ref:`FLTMODE_CH<FLTMODE_CH>` to the channel you are using)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:304
#: 580247413f224c3e9eefc78331fc678c
msgid "Before you takeoff and fly with geo-fencing make sure all the parameters are setup as described above, and also make sure you have a good GPS lock. If you lose GPS lock then geo-fencing will disable itself until GPS lock is regained, so don't use it if your GPS signal is marginal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:309
#: 45215fc39c5d4519b2c14bcd79455966
msgid "I'd also recommend you test it gently at first. Try slowly approaching a fence boundary and ensure it correctly 'bounces' off the virtual wall and returns to the return point OK. Then after taking control again, try slowly approaching the minimum altitude and ensure it bounces off the ``FENCE_MINALT`` you have set."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:315
#: 1e831297f85648d9b091b344c6e4fcc3
msgid "While developing geo-fencing I found that combining it with MANUAL mode is the most fun. It gives you all of the excitement of manual flight with sharp turns and fancy stunts while saving your plane when you make a mistake."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:321
#: 5b616638b59d43d2916e9bef330a8e45
msgid "Example flight"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:323
#: ce3265ad821349e3bc939a345a29b05e
msgid "This is the track from a flight with geo-fencing enabled at my local flying club while flying my !SkyWalker. The white lines show the geo-fence boundary, plus you can see the return point in the middle. You can also see the points where the plane breached the geo-fence to the north, west and south. There were also numerous altitude breaches, as I was using this flight to try to improve my inverted flight skills in MANUAL mode. The plane would not have survived without the geo-fence!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:334
#: b8d2926e5e334655b3df8185eaa2bdd5
msgid "Notice that the geo-fence in this example runs along the middle of the runway. This is to conform to my local club rules. The takeoff and landing were done with the fence disabled. I had ``FENCE_CHANNEL`` set to 7, and ``RST_SWITCH_CH`` set to 6. That allowed me to enable the fence after takeoff using one switch, then to take back control after a breach using the spring loaded trainer switch."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:341
#: a5290a23c9834366808937f65ce004a4
msgid "in firmware versions 4.1 and later, ``FENCE_CHANNEL`` is replaced by setting a channel's ``RCx_OPTION`` to 208, and ``RST_SWITCH_CH`` is replaced by setting a channel's ``RCx_OPTION`` to 96."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:344
#: 4359a5852db84036972f7b0ba3a31039
msgid "MAVLink support"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:346
#: 9b84dab5259747d099367a5013c12b45
msgid "ArduPilot will report the fence status via the MAVLink GCS protocol. The key status packet is called FENCE_STATUS, and is defined in \"ardupilotmega.xml\". A typical FENCE_STATUSpacket looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:354
#: 326a50884c0749779d03ad09ad1a187f
msgid "The breach_status field is 0 if inside the fence, and 1 if outside. The breach_count is how many fence breaches you have had on this flight. The breach_type is the type of the last breach (see the FENCE_BREACH enum in ardupilotmega.xml). The breach_time is the time in milliseconds of the breach since APM was booted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:360
#: 7de4d2d0dc6f4635ac6c19e547099ff6
msgid "The MAV_SYS_STATUS_GEOFENCE bit of the MAV_SYS_STATUS_SENSOR portion of the SYS_STATUS message indicates whether or not the geo-fence is breached.  As of this writing only the MAVProxy GCS recognizes this status bit and reports the status of the geo-fence.  In the future the Mission Planner, APM Planner, and other GCS applications should get support for announcing geo-fence status during the flight."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:367
#: 1ae564c381f14af890cb26e34993a4e9
msgid "The MAV_CMD_DO_FENCE_ENABLE MAVLink command message allows a GCS to enable or disable a fence interactively.  As of this writing only MAVProxy supports this message using the \"fence enable\" or \"fence disable\" commands.  In the future Mission Planner, APM Planner, and other GCS applications should get support for interactively enabling and disabling the geo-fence without needing to use a manual transmitter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/geofencing.rst:375
#: 09897bdcef264a4f800480d51ca0c46d
msgid "Advanced Features"
msgstr ""

